\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath}
\title{Contrastive Objectives for Recommendation}
\author{T. Molina}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Method}
We optimize the loss $L = L_{rec} + \lambda L_{cl}$, where $L_{rec}$ is the rating reconstruction term and $L_{cl}$ is the contrastive term. In ablations we set $\mathcal{L} = L_{rec}$ to isolate the effect of contrastive learning. Minimizing $L$ aligns user and item embeddings in a shared space. The gradient of $\mathcal{L}$ with respect to the user embedding has a closed form. Empirically, training with $L$ converges within twenty epochs.
\section{Experiments}
We report recall and NDCG on three public datasets.
\end{document}
